@Begin

Metadata: 
Patient: Margaret, 74 years old
Diagnosis: Early-stage Alzheimer's disease
Language Profile: Word-finding pauses, some repetition, generally coherent

Dr. Chen: *CLI* Good morning, Margaret. It's nice to meet you today. Can you tell me a little bit about yourself? What do you like to do in your free time?

Margaret: *PAT* Oh, um... I like to garden. I have a big garden at my house, it's called... uh... Willowdale. I have a lot of flowers there.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* Willowdale, that sounds lovely. Do you have a wife or children who help you with the garden?

Margaret: *PAT* I have... uh... a granddaughter who comes to visit me every weekend. Her name is... uh... Emily. She's very nice.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* That's wonderful. It's great to have family support. Can you tell me about your husband? What was his name?

Margaret: *PAT* Oh... uh... his name was... uh... Reginald. He was a big man. He had a... uh... a mustache.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* A mustache, that's interesting. Did you two have any children?

Margaret: *PAT* Yes... uh... we had two children. One boy and one girl. Their names were... uh... David and Sarah. I think.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* That's okay, Margaret. It's not uncommon for people with Alzheimer's to have trouble with names and faces. Can you tell me about a time when you felt happy or fulfilled?

Margaret: *PAT* Oh... uh... I used to love to cook. I would make all sorts of dishes for my family. My favorite was... uh... beef stew.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* Beef stew, that sounds delicious. Have you ever traveled to any exciting places?

Margaret: *PAT* Yes... uh... I went to... uh... Paris once. I saw the Eiffel Tower. It was... uh... very big.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* Paris, that's wonderful. Did you have a favorite part of the trip?

Margaret: *PAT* Oh... uh... I liked the... uh... the park. I had a picnic there.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* A picnic, that sounds like a lovely way to spend time. Margaret, can you tell me about a time when you felt scared or overwhelmed?

Margaret: *PAT* Oh... uh... I was afraid of... uh... the dark. I wouldn't go to bed until... uh... my lights were on.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* That's understandable. It's natural to feel scared or overwhelmed at times. Margaret, is there anything you'd like to talk about or any questions you have for me?

Margaret: *PAT* No... uh... I don't know. I'm just... uh... a little confused sometimes. That's all.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* It's okay, Margaret. We can work through that together. I'm here to support you. Let's schedule a follow-up appointment for next week, and we'll go over some things again.

Margaret: *PAT* Okay... uh... I'll try.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* Great, I'll see you then. Margaret, do you have any questions for me?

Margaret: *PAT* No... uh... I don't think so.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* Okay, well, let's get started on the next part of our evaluation. Can you please stand up and walk over to the chair?

Margaret: *PAT* Oh... uh... I don't know if I can do that. My legs feel... uh... a little weak.

Dr. Chen: *CLI* That's okay, Margaret. We can take it slow. Let's start with just standing up from the couch.

Margaret: *PAT* Okay... uh... I'll try.

@End
